      "title": "Fux1 VAE",
      "properties": {
        "ue_properties": {
          "widget_ue_connectable": {},
          "version": "7.8",
          "input_ue_unconnectable": {}
        }
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "\n====================================================================\n🧠 TECH EXPLAINER: FLUX VAE COMPATIBILITY WITH Z-IMAGE\n====================================================================\nThe short answer: Both model architectures share the exact same \nmathematical latent DNA (16-channel architecture).\n\n1. THE INHERENT LATENT MATCH (16-CHANNEL MATRIX)\nZ-Image is structurally built on top of the Lumina-Next pipeline. \nJust like FLUX, this modern architecture utilizes an advanced \nAutoencoder (VAE) operating with exactly 16 latent channels. \nIn comparison, legacy models like SD1.5 or SDXL only use 4 channels. \nBecause the mathematical structure of the latent vectors is identical, \nthe FLUX VAE can map, read, and decompress the data generated by \nZ-Image flawlessly.\n\n2. WHY YOU SHOULD CHOOSE THE FLUX VAE (`ae.safetensors`)\n* High-Fidelity Reconstruction: The FLUX VAE is one of the most advanced \n  autoencoders available. It processes micro-details like text, eye reflections, \n  and fine cybernetic circuit lines significantly cleaner than older models.\n* Color & Contrast Correction: The native Lumina VAE sometimes suffers \n  from washed-out color grading or contrast shifting during fast Turbo passes. \n  Swapping it for the FLUX VAE automatically corrects these artifacts during \n  the decode step.\n\n3. VRAM ADVANTAGE ON THE TESLA P4\nBecause ComfyUI loads the VAE model at the very end of the execution loop \n(strictly for decoding the latent matrix back into a viewable PNG), you \ngain massive structural clarity without bottlenecking your KSampler's \nVRAM buffer during the heavy diffusion phase.\n====================================================================\n"
      ],
      "color": "#2a363b",
      "bgcolor": "#3f5159"
    },
