      "title": "⚠️ Model Sampling Aura Flow - Bypassed",
      "properties": {
        "ue_properties": {
          "widget_ue_connectable": {},
          "version": "7.8",
          "input_ue_unconnectable": {}
        }
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "⚙️ PARAMETER EXPLAINER: MODEL SAMPLING & SHIFT (3.00)\n\nWhy is this node in the graph, and why is it currently set to BYPASS?\n\n1. WHAT DOES THE \"SHIFT\" VALUE DO?\nModern Flow-Matching architectures (like AuraFlow or FLUX) use a \"Shift\" \nparameter to manipulate the timestep schedule. A higher shift value \n(like 3.00) forces the KSampler to focus its computational power on the \nearly, high-noise steps. This allows the model to generate crisp, high-contrast \nimages in a very low number of steps (e.g., 4 to 8 steps).\n\n2. WHY ARE WE NOT USING IT HERE? (BYPASS MODE)\nLook closely at the graph: the \"ModelSamplingAuraFlow\" node is muted/greyed out. \nThe lila MODEL pipeline passes straight through it because of two reasons:\n-> Architectual Mismatch: We are running \"z_image_turbo\", which is built on \n   the Lumina-Next DNA. Lumina has its own baked-in turbo inference logic.\n-> Artifact Prevention: Forcing an AuraFlow timestep curve onto a Lumina model \n   would distort the geometry, causing heavy contrast glitches or deep noise.\n\n3. THE WORKFLOW TAKEAWAY:\nWe keep the node on Bypass to bypass the math completely. This ensures the \npure GGUF model stream feeds directly into the KSampler, utilizing 100% of \nZ-Image's native speed on our 8GB Tesla P4 card.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
